    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some testing data for a new high-performance braking setup. Can you compare the thermal dissipation rates and heat soak characteristics of ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads versus standard semi-metallic pads during a high-speed deceleration event? I'm specifically interested in how the thermal gradient affects the rotor surface during repeated heavy braking cycles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads exhibit superior thermal dissipation rates compared to standard semi-metallic pads due to their higher thermal conductivity and lower heat capacity. During aggressive deceleration events, CMCs cool down faster, reducing the risk of heat soak and maintaining consistent braking performance over repeated heavy braking cycles. In contrast, semi-metallic pads tend to have higher thermal gradients on the rotor surface, resulting in uneven wear and potential hot spots, which could compromise rotor lifespan and braking efficiency. The faster cooling of CMCs translates to a smoother thermal gradient across the rotor and better performance under similar conditions.",
      "source": "target",
