    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One issue we've been seeing in the last few test runs is that the semi-metallic pads seem to be \"acting up\" unpredictably under extreme heat. Even when the cooling calculations look right, there's this intermittent instability in the friction coefficient that we can't quite pin down. What could cause that kind of erratic behavior in the material during high-stress phases?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Intermittent instability in the friction coefficient of semi-metallic pads during high-stress phases may be related to several factors. One potential cause could be the phase transformation of ferrous compounds within the semi-metallic matrix which occurs at high temperatures, potentially altering the pad's friction characteristics. Thermal inconsistencies can also lead to transient microstructural changes, whereby heat concentrates in certain areas, resulting in \"hot spots\" that impact the friction consistently. Additionally, the presence of different alloys or additives could further exacerbate these inconsistencies due to their variable melting points, thermal expansion coefficients, or stress relief effects. It is important to consider the detailed composition of the semi-metallic pads, as well as the temperature-correlated wear patterns on the rotor, to comprehensively understand this phenomenon. Advanced temperature mapping and friction coefficient analysis during a controlled test could help identify the precise cause of the instability.",
      "source": "target",
